<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stanisław August Poniatowski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Stanisław II Augustus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stanisław August Poniatowski was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, elected in 1764 with the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. A patron of the arts and of reform, he supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>, but he could not prevent the three partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and he abdicated in 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski.jpg" alt="Stanisław August Poniatowski" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcello Bacciarelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanis%C5%82aw_II_August_Poniatowski_in_coronation_clothes.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stanisław Antoni Poniatowski was born on 17 January 1732 at Wołczyn, now Vowchyn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. He was the fourth son of Count Stanisław Poniatowski, Castellan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, and Princess Konstancja Czartoryska, and one of eight surviving children. In his twenties he travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where he made friends such as the British diplomat Charles Hanbury Williams, and he sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> as a deputy. In 1755, aged 22, he reached the Russian court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> as a diplomat and became closely involved with the future empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> in 1763, Catherine supported Poniatowski, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> spent about 2.5 million rubles on his election. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> elected him king on 7 September 1764 with 5,584 votes, and he was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 25 November. Contrary to expectations he tried to reform the Commonwealth, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, Russia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> wished to keep it weak, and conservative nobles saw reform as a threat to their liberties. At the Sejm of 1766 he tried to restrict the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a> and failed. The War of the Bar Confederation (1768–1772) led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772.</p><p>In the 1780s Poniatowski could not pass even small reforms, because he failed to confederate the sejms and so protect them from the veto. The Great Sejm of 1788–1792 produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>. Conservatives formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/targowica-confederation">Targowica Confederation</a>, allied with Catherine, and Russian troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1792. The royal army, of only about 37,000 men, was commanded by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Józef Poniatowski</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a>. After the victory at Zieleńce the king founded the Order of Virtuti Militari. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> followed in 1793.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Poniatowski did not encourage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a> of 1794 but supported it once it began. After its defeat he was told to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a>, and he signed his abdication on 25 November 1795, a month after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a>. He ended his life as a captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and died on 12 February 1798.</p><p>He remains controversial. Critics blame his lack of resolve against the partitions, while others see a skilful statesman who showed courage in passing the Constitution. He was the most important patron of the arts of the Polish Enlightenment: he founded the National Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, remodelled the Royal Castle and built the Łazienki Palace, and he made possible the Commission of National Education.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stanisław August Poniatowski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Augustus II and Augustus III of the Saxon Wettin dynasty owed their thrones in large part to Russian arms, and Catherine secured the election of <b>Stanisław August Poniatowski</b> in 1764, while Russia used the grievances of Orthodox and Protestant dissenters as a pretext for intervention.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry of Valois, elected in 1573, was the first king so chosen and <b>Stanisław August Poniatowski</b>, elected in 1764, the last; the Constitution of 3 May 1791 made the throne hereditary, but Poland–Lithuania was partitioned out of existence by 1795.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vowchyn, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.29,23.31&amp;year=1774" aria-label="Vowchyn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5074 359 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stanisław August Poniatowski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5524 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5524 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.29,23.31&amp;year=1774">What was here in 1774? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
